Lag + tabs plan (read-only; nothing built). Based on reading both code bases; nothing measured on his PC.

NAMING: the smooth one is my v1 (Go shell + WebView2 + MuPDF-WASM, source in ~/pdfapp/web/app.js, engine.js), not a Tauri app; the Tauri one in my notes is his FocusForge project. I assume he means v1, tell me if he means FocusForge.

HOW THE TWO RENDER (from code)
- v1 (smooth): each page is a <div> sized to the page; an IntersectionObserver with 400 px margin triggers renderPage only for pages near the viewport (app.js line 61); the page is rasterised by MuPDF-WASM, encoded to PNG (engine.js lines 19-28) and shown as an image, so scrolling is the browser compositor moving already-decoded bitmaps on the GPU, never blocked by our code; rendering is async (awaits yield to the UI), prefetches 400 px ahead, and text/link overlays are DOM elements.
- Current egui app (laggy): every frame the app redraws all visible pages itself, and rendering is synchronous on the UI thread (app.rs page_tex, around lines 95-115): when a page comes into view or zoom settles, the frame blocks while MuPDF rasterises (to_pixmap), converts RGB to RGBA (ColorImage::from_rgb) and uploads the texture. Page raster time in my own OCR timing lines was 60-160 ms for a 1654x2339 page on a weak 2-core sandbox (a proxy; on his HiDPI laptop pages are rendered at zoom*pixels-per-point so likely larger, but his CPU is far faster). Several pages become visible at once after a zoom settle, so a zoom ends in a 0.2-0.5 s freeze; scrolling hits one stall every time a new page edge appears, because there is no prefetch.
 Other code-verified costs: (a) texture cache is cleared completely when it holds more than 10 pages (app.rs "if self.tex.len() > 10 { self.tex.clear(); }"), which thrashes at higher zoom or with a tall window; (b) while text is selected, every frame clones the page's whole character list (page_chars(i).clone()) just to paint the highlight; (c) every mouse move scans all chars of the hovered page for the text cursor and all links for the hand cursor, and the first hover on a page extracts its text synchronously (tens of ms); (d) thumbnails render one at a time on the UI thread; (e) set_doc loads every page just to read its size on open (339 page loads for his book), a one-off multi-second hitch on open; (f) texture upload of 4-8 MP pages per frame.
 Not the cause (checked): release profile is opt-level 3, LTO, codegen-units 1; the app is repaint-on-demand, not a busy loop.
 Unknown until measured: GPU used by the window. His OCR got 5-8x faster after Windows set litepdf.exe to High performance, which means he is on a hybrid laptop; the window's OpenGL then may run on whichever GPU Windows picks, so the same setting may matter for UI smoothness too. Please ask him whether the UI got smoother after that change.

PLAN (ranked by benefit/risk)
1. Never render on the frame thread. A render worker thread with its own MuPDF document (MuPDF contexts are not shared between threads), fed by a request queue: visible pages first, then 1-2 pages ahead in the scroll direction, then thumbnails. The UI thread only uploads finished bitmaps (one or two per frame, with a ~4 ms budget) and keeps drawing the old texture scaled until the new one arrives (this already happens during zoom; it would also happen after settle). For documents with unsaved edits, the worker must see them: after each edit, pages touched are re-rendered from the in-memory document on the UI thread (rare, single page) and a fresh snapshot (save_fast) is written for the worker in the background; simple version first: worker reads the current file snapshot, edited pages use the UI-thread path.
2. Progressive quality: first a quick low-res render (about 0.35x, ~10-20 ms) so a new page is never blank, then the full-res; upload without the RGB-to-RGBA copy where possible.
3. Cache by bytes (e.g. 300-400 MB of textures, least-recently-used) instead of "clear all above 10".
4. Cheap per-frame work: store selection highlight rectangles once per selection, not per frame; build a per-page line index so hover tests are O(log n); extract page text on the worker too (first hover shows no cursor change until ready).
5. Lazy page sizes: read the first page's size on open, fetch the rest in the background (layout uses the first size until known), so opening a 339-page book is instant.
6. Zoom: keep the stretch-the-old-texture behaviour, render the new zoom level on the worker, swap in when ready (no freeze). Optional: ctrl+wheel anchor stays as is.
7. A built-in perf log (written to %APPDATA%\LitePDF\perf.log): frame time max/avg per second, render ms per page and queue length, texture uploads per frame. This is how we verify on his PC; unverified until he runs it.
Expected result: scrolling and zoom never freeze above ~16 ms; pages appear blurry for a moment then sharpen (same trade v1 makes). Cannot be verified here beyond compile + logic tests (no OpenGL under Wine); I can run a headless frame-time test on the host with the software egui backend to catch regressions, but only his PC proves smoothness.

TABS plan
- State: today everything (doc, sizes, texture cache, links, text cache, selection, scroll, zoom, find, dirty flag, path, undo) lives directly in App. Introduce a TabState struct holding those; App keeps "active" fields as now and swaps them in/out on tab switch (swap, not rewrite every method), so the change is mostly additive.
- UI: a tab strip under the menu bar; each tab has the file name (a "*" when unsaved) and an x; a "+" opens a file dialog; middle-click closes; Ctrl+Tab / Ctrl+Shift+Tab cycle; Ctrl+W closes; Ctrl+O and drag-and-drop open in a new tab; opening an already open file focuses its tab; closing an unsaved tab asks (Save / Don't save / Cancel). The window title shows the active tab's name (already done).
- Memory/perf: inactive tabs keep the document but drop textures; each tab has its own render worker queue entry (workers share one thread).
- Restore: remember last open files? Not by default (no surprise on start) unless he asks.
- OCR/AI/compress dialogs operate on the active tab. A running OCR job pins its tab (cannot be closed until it finishes or is cancelled).
Effort estimate (build + host tests): lag fixes 1.5-2 h, tabs 1-1.5 h. I would do lag first (the render queue is a prerequisite for tabs too, since each tab has its own caches), then tabs, in one build or two installers, whichever he prefers.
Verified here: code paths, proxy render time from OCR timing lines (60-160 ms/page, weak sandbox). Not verified: any frame time on his PC, whether the High-performance GPU setting changes the UI, how much of his lag is the freeze vs the GPU context.
EOF
